{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claim asserts the panel's reliability gain comes 'from averaging independent errors rather than from the checklist per se.' But this creates a confound the target never resolves: it cannot distinguish genuine reliability gain (independent errors averaged out) from mere checklist compliance that tracks the right answer (correlated errors that happen to hit the target). Averaging independent errors is a statistical mechanism; checklist-compliance that tracks the right answer is a correlated-error mechanism. The problem demands evidence that distinguishes the two within a single evaluation, but the target's framework—stressing error-averaging while relegating the checklist to a cue-encoding boundary condition—provides no single-evaluation operationalization to separate them. An evaluation showing the panel outperforming the expert would be equally consistent with both mechanisms under this framing.","target":"3a7ff4fb033ac3cbcef9d9cc7631b28a182d2c8fc38215a24ce2bf0bc82b7046"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target's central identifying criterion—'low base-rate outcome'—is a population-level property of the task, not a within-evaluation diagnostic that separates genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance. The problem asks what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish a genuine gain from compliance that happens to track the right answer. But knowing the task has a low base rate does not, by itself, distinguish a panel that genuinely encodes rare-event cue-weightings from one that merely defaults to the base rate (and thus tracks the right answer by being systematically conservative). The ideal-observer analogy the target relies on actually undercuts its ability to make this discrimination: both 'genuine diagnostic encoding' and 'regression to the mean' predict correct answers on common events and failures on rare events, so the target's variance-vs-bias framing cannot operationally separate the two within one evaluation.","target":"a713865c22a110966a290ab75347ac1537a9589b83a65fed5f310296eeb6a95a"}]}